<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Han</h1><p class="st-meta">947 CE – 951 CE · capital Kaifeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The shortest of the Five Dynasties, set up at Kaifeng by the Shatuo general Liu Zhiyuan after the Khitan withdrew from northern China in 947.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Khitan army of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin</a> (936-947) at the start of 947, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedong-shanxi">Hedong</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, proclaimed himself emperor. As the Khitan withdrew north under popular resistance, he entered Kaifeng and took over the old court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Later Han relied on the palace army and on ruthless ministers, and its tax collectors and courts were notorious for harshness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a> died in 948, leaving his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chengyou">Liu Chengyou</a> under a group of senior ministers and generals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chengyou">Liu Chengyou</a> had leading ministers killed and plotted against the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a>, who marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. The emperor was killed in the fighting, and early in 951 Guo Wei founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan's</a> brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chong">Liu Chong</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-han">Northern Han</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-han.jpg" alt="Later Han" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Han.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>947 CE – 951 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaifeng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Zhiyuan (Emperor Gaozu), Liu Chengyou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Han (Five Dynasties), Han (Liu Zhiyuan)</dd></dl></aside></div>
